From 85c27427cb4b249223f5dac0cb099c8e91a9763d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Selfbus IP interface <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 00:27:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Do not hand out uninitialised heap from propertyValueRead()

BauSystemB::propertyValueRead() allocates the buffer for the
requested number of elements and reports that size in `length`,
whatever readProperty() actually wrote. readProperty() sets
elementCount to 0 when the property does not exist, and
Property::read() returns 0 when the requested range is not
available - in both cases the buffer is never written.

A caller that trusts `length` then reads uninitialised heap. In
our case a read of PID_FRIENDLY_NAME on an unprogrammed device
returned the remains of an unrelated string, which the firmware
duly published over its web API.

elementCount already carries the answer; this reports the size
that belongs to it.
---
 src/knx/bau_systemB.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/knx/bau_systemB.cpp b/src/knx/bau_systemB.cpp
index b727148..b07d7b8 100644
--- a/src/knx/bau_systemB.cpp
+++ b/src/knx/bau_systemB.cpp
@@ -744,6 +744,13 @@ void BauSystemB::propertyValueRead(ObjectType objectType, uint8_t objectInstance
 
         *data = new uint8_t [size];
         obj->readProperty((PropertyID)propertyId, startIndex, elementCount, *data);
+
+        // readProperty() reports what it really wrote. Keeping the requested
+        // size here would hand the caller uninitialised heap memory.
+        if (elementCount == 0)
+            size = 0;
+        else if (startIndex > 0)
+            size = elementSize * elementCount;
     }
     else
     {
